# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signaling / authority server configuration. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Signs room + session tokens. If unset the server
# generates a random secret at boot, which means every restart invalidates all
# outstanding tokens (fine for dev, breaks reconnects in production).
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
JWT_SECRET=

PORT=3001

# Comma-separated allowlist of browser origins permitted to reach the server.
# Leave UNSET for LAN / tunnel testing (the server then reflects the request
# origin). ALWAYS set it in production.
# CLIENT_ORIGIN=https://app.example.com,https://www.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TURN relay. Without this, calls fail on symmetric NAT / mobile carriers and
# strict corporate firewalls (roughly 8-15% of real-world peers).
#
# Pick ONE of the three blocks below. Both can be set at once — the server
# merges them and the browser tries whichever answers first — but one is enough.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# (1) Metered.ca — hosted, has a free tier, nothing to run.
#     Sign up at https://www.metered.ca, then in the dashboard:
#       - the app name on the home page is METERED_APP_NAME (it forms the API
#         host: https://<appname>.metered.live)
#       - TURN -> Credentials -> create one, and copy its API key here
#     The key is per-credential, so revoking it in the dashboard is enough to
#     cut off access; it never reaches the browser either way.
# METERED_APP_NAME=
# METERED_API_KEY=
#     Paid plans only. On the free plan leave this unset — you get the shared
#     `standard.relay.metered.ca` host and a region here would be rejected.
# METERED_REGION=
#     Seconds to reuse a fetched credential list before asking Metered again.
# METERED_CACHE_SECONDS=600

# (2) Self-hosted coturn with the REST API. `TURN_SECRET` must equal coturn's
#     `static-auth-secret` so the server can mint short-lived HMAC credentials
#     instead of shipping a static password.
# TURN_URLS=turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turns:turn.example.com:5349?transport=tcp
# TURN_SECRET=
# TURN_TTL_SECONDS=86400

# (3) Static credentials, for a relay with no REST API. Works with any provider
#     that just gives you a username and password, Metered included.
# TURN_URLS=
# TURN_USERNAME=
# TURN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Persistence: where room artifacts (code / notes / whiteboard / layout) are
# stored so a room survives everyone leaving and the server restarting.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DOC_STORE_DIR=.data
# Rooms with no activity for this long are evicted from memory and disk.
ROOM_TTL_HOURS=72
# Hard cap on a single room's persisted artifact size (bytes).
DOC_MAX_BYTES=8388608

# Render + Firebase: durable cloud saving (recommended on ephemeral free hosts).
# DOC_STORE_PROVIDER=firestore
# DOC_FLUSH_DEBOUNCE_MS=10000
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=facetimeos
# FIRESTORE_DATABASE_ID=(default)
# Upload the private JSON as a Render Secret File, not into this repository.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/etc/secrets/firebase-service-account.json
# Alternatively set FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON in Render's private env UI.
# Never set both credential methods. Never expose either as NEXT_PUBLIC_*.
# CLIENT_ORIGIN=https://facetimeos.vercel.app,ftos://app
# In Firestore mode ROOM_TTL_HOURS only evicts memory, not cloud-saved artifacts.
